@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+# Actual node over WSS: local acceptance
+
+The local nginx candidate now carries actual efsn reports through the collector,
+snapshot writer, PostgreSQL, HTTPS API and compiled Edge browser. The test runs
+the existing synthetic sixty-block chain with ten transactions per block, public
+test key 1, no peers and no ongoing mining. It uses the 8-node capacity settings,
+64 KiB head budget and 4/1/1 MiB input/output/snapshot candidate with one reporter.
+The prior eight-reporter profile remains separate evidence.
+
+The test compares the head and fifty history blocks with direct RPC, preserves
+transaction hashes and chart counts, checks ticket/peer/pending/mining values,
+and exercises four browser checkpoints: current head, pinned head, expired data
+after stopping the writer, and automatic recovery after restarting it. Writer
+loss does not change the stationary node's head. Collector loss during actual
+mining remains a separate required drill.
+
+All timestamp-producing dashboard processes run on the same Linux host. The
+first integration layout mixed Linux collector receipts with a Windows writer;
+one receipt was 637 ms later than its snapshot observation, correctly yielding
+unknown report freshness. Moving the writer beside the collector and API fixes
+the fixture layout without widening timestamp validation. Keep the single-host
+deployment assumption. Any later multi-host setup needs clock synchronization
+and explicit clock-failure acceptance.
+
+The efsn fixture uses an explicit `wss://127.0.0.1:port` URL, so its normal reporter
+cannot fall back to plaintext. A temporary CA is supplied through Go's
+`SSL_CERT_FILE`; the production dialer and certificate verification are unchanged.
+Node HTTPS checks use that CA and record an authorized TLS 1.3 session. A fresh
+Edge process first rejects the untrusted test certificate. The functional browser
+check then uses a temporary browser profile with an exception pinned to this
+test leaf's SHA-256 SPKI. It does not disable all HTTPS checks or alter system
+trust. This verifies browser transport/functionality, not acceptance of a publicly
+issued certificate; issuance, renewal and public trust still need deployment checks.
+The [Chromium switch implementation](https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/%2B/8402d3c5bc13e018fa75eba650ed881755e0223b%5E%21/)
+describes that scoped test exception.
+
+The Linux test uses PostgreSQL 16.15, Ubuntu nginx 1.24.0-2ubuntu7.18, Node 22.11.0,
+pg 8.23.1 and Edge 154.0.4258.48. PostgreSQL is extracted into scratch and started
+as a fresh private SCRAM-authenticated loopback cluster. The original plain-loopback
+test also passes against Windows PostgreSQL 18.6. These are recorded test inputs,
+not a selected production support matrix.
+
+Opt in through the existing `test/efsn-telemetry.test.cjs` by supplying
+`EFSN_TLS_CA`, `EFSN_TLS_CERT`, `EFSN_TLS_KEY`, `EFSN_TLS_OUTPUT`, `EFSN_TLS_SPKI`
+and `TEST_NGINX` in addition to its existing database, Go binary, Linux Node,
+Playwright and evidence settings. CA/certificate/output/nginx/password paths are
+Windows paths mapped into WSL; the private key path is already a Linux path.
+The Go fixture binary must permit loopback WSS. Linux collector, writer and API
+use the same PostgreSQL cluster; Windows controls the fixture and browser.
+
+No application, dependency, schema, frontend or nginx-template changes were
+required. Changes are restricted to test orchestration, TLS checks and orderly
+cleanup. The writer controller's stop operation is idempotent, and cleanup stops
+the node and writer before closing collector/proxy processes. All 173 contracts,
+13 socket tests, the actual WSS scenario and original plain-loopback scenario pass.
